   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Jobs: masculine and feminine",
     "texto": [
      "Many jobs form the feminine by adding **-in**: **der Lehrer** → **die Lehrerin** (teacher), **der Koch** → **die Köchin** (cook). There are spelling changes and some jobs have their own forms, so it's worth learning each pair.",
      "Unlike English («I'm a teacher»), German doesn't use an article for your job: **Ich bin Lehrerin**. With an adjective, one does appear: **Ich bin eine gute Lehrerin** (I'm a good teacher)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Working «als» and «bei»",
     "texto": [
      "To say what job you do: **Ich arbeite als Koch** (I work as a cook). No article.",
      "To say where you work: **Ich arbeite bei einer Bank** (I work at a bank). With «bei» you use the **dative**: **einer** is the feminine dative of «eine»."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
